WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:30.071
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. On today's episode — the Fraser Canyon's effectively cut in half, with Highway 1 and Highway 3 both closed as wildfires burn right up against that corridor. Plus, our Deep Dive asks the hard question hiding behind this region's own name: what's actually happening to real caribou herds, and whether "recovery" is still the right word for some of them. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:55.371 --> 00:01:20.228
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Friday, July 24th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces & Outdoor Life.

00:01:21.528 --> 00:01:42.099
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're deep into fire season right now, so today's roundup leans hard into what's actually burning, what's holding steady, and what that means for anyone living or travelling through this landscape — before we shift into the wildlife side of things in the Deep Dive.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:02:12.827
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Out at Horsefly Lake it's fifteen degrees this morning with clear skies, headed for a high of twenty-seven and a low of fourteen tonight, with some drizzle expected tomorrow. Across the rest of the region — 100 Mile House sitting at sixteen, Williams Lake at eighteen, Quesnel at seventeen, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Riske Creek also at seventeen. Clear skies everywhere.

00:02:13.127 --> 00:02:24.269
<v Riley>It's a hot, dry stretch region-wide. Stay hydrated, and if you're heading anywhere near an active fire zone, check the BC Wildfire Dashboard before you go.

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:28.497
<v Casey>Let's get into what's actually happening out there.

00:02:28.797 --> 00:02:55.796
<v Casey>The Fraser Canyon is effectively cut in half right now. Highway 1 through the canyon remains closed, and Highway 3 east of Hope is shut down too, both because of active wildfire. The Norwegian Creek fire is burning right up against that Highway 3 corridor — the Tribune published a photo of it from July 21st, two days before the closure took effect.

00:02:57.296 --> 00:03:26.010
<v Riley>That's not an isolated flare-up. Global News is reporting homes and structures destroyed as multiple fires burn out of control across the province, including what's being called the Brunswick Creek Complex — three fires tearing through the Fraser Canyon together. One farm owner near North Bend has already lost buildings, and by the sounds of the coverage, the last few days have been devastating for that family.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:35.738
<v Casey>And that highway closure isn't a minor inconvenience — it's the main freight and travel corridor between the coast and the Interior.

00:03:36.038 --> 00:03:59.180
<v Riley>Right, and layered on top of that, My Cariboo Now is reporting that provincial officials are bracing for worsening drought conditions on top of the fire risk. Low snowpack this spring, a dry June, now a hot July — that's exactly the fuel-and-drought combination that keeps crews stretched thin for weeks at a time.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:18.251
<v Casey>Closer to home, there's a genuinely nice story out of Clinton. A local business there has been feeding BC Wildfire Service crews stationed in the area — Maria Kosovic and her team putting together meals for firefighters working long shifts nearby.

00:04:18.551 --> 00:04:34.836
<v Riley>That's the kind of logistics support that never makes the incident command briefing but keeps a crew fed and functional on a fourteen-hour shift. Somebody has to think about lunch when everyone else is thinking about containment lines.

00:04:35.136 --> 00:04:57.850
<v Casey>Down in Castlegar, the Wildfire Service has put an area restriction in place around the Matthew Creek fire — that's part of the larger Bootleg Complex, now estimated at 869 hectares. Not our immediate backyard, but it's the same complex-fire pattern we're seeing across the southern Interior this week, just a different valley.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:19.492
<v Riley>And it's not staying contained to fire zones themselves. Kelowna's Air Quality Health Index hit the very top of the scale on Thursday — ten-plus, the highest risk category there is — with wildfire smoke blanketing the city and prompting health warnings for anyone with respiratory issues.

00:05:19.792 --> 00:05:27.506
<v Casey>People planning a weekend trip through the Okanagan should check air quality before they check the trailhead map.

00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:52.234
<v Riley>The Narwhal took a step back this week and looked at the bigger political picture behind all of this — two decades of federal promises, going back to the Harper years right through to Carney now, that carbon capture technology would slow Canada's emissions curve, while wildfire seasons keep getting measurably worse regardless of who's in office.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:06:15.291
<v Casey>And in Ontario, the political fallout from a rough wildfire season is already surfacing. Ricochet did a written Q&A — over email, since that's the only way this show does interviews — with NDP leader Marit Stiles, who's calling for a public inquiry into the provincial government's wildfire response this year.

00:06:15.591 --> 00:06:35.733
<v Riley>Worth being clear that's a different accountability question than what we usually track here — that's about provincial emergency response chains of command in Ontario, not a BC story. But the throughline connects: governments promising technological fixes while fire seasons keep outrunning the timeline on those promises.

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:57.033
<v Casey>A couple of stories landed under today's tag that don't really belong with the fire coverage but got swept in anyway. Nvidia donated a DGX GB300 supercomputer to the US Naval Postgraduate School this week — training simulations, digital twins, weather prediction modelling. Interesting hardware story, zero Cariboo relevance.

00:06:57.333 --> 00:07:11.047
<v Riley>And somewhere in the algorithm's mind, a new Resident Evil trailer counted as "wild spaces" content because eagle-eyed fans spotted video game easter eggs in it. We'll leave that one entirely alone.

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:33.204
<v Casey>Kamloops did have a genuinely outdoor event worth a mention, though — Riverside Park hosted the International Buskers Festival this week, performers from around the world bringing street theatre and music to the riverside. That's the kind of outdoor programming that actually belongs on this theme, even without a fire angle.

00:07:34.404 --> 00:08:04.403
<v Riley>Switching gears now — a handful of stories closer to home that matter for very different reasons. Premier Eby was talking tough on tariff negotiations with the US this week, saying BC needs to be ready to carry "big sticks" at the table, while Prime Minister Carney is keeping his cards close on the federal strategy. The Tribune's covering how that posture plays out for BC industries — mills included.

00:08:04.703 --> 00:08:20.131
<v Casey>Worth watching closely if you're in the forestry sector and wondering how the next round of trade talks lands on log exports and softwood lumber pricing. That conversation directly touches jobs in Quesnel and Williams Lake.

00:08:20.431 --> 00:08:36.716
<v Riley>100 Mile House emergency room closures are nearing a record number this year, according to My Cariboo Now — another strain point for rural healthcare capacity that keeps showing up news cycle after news cycle, regardless of season.

00:08:38.216 --> 00:08:58.787
<v Casey>And a genuinely sobering number worth sitting with for a second: the BC Coroners Service says accidental drownings are up more than fifty percent from last year. Forty people died in accidental drownings between May 1st and July 16th, compared with twenty-six over the same stretch in 2025.

00:08:59.087 --> 00:09:14.515
<v Riley>That's exactly the kind of statistic that belongs squarely under "outdoor life" — hotter summers mean more people on rivers and lakes, and water safety habits and life jacket use haven't caught up with that shift.

00:09:14.815 --> 00:09:35.386
<v Casey>Highway 7 drivers near Hope should also expect delays this week — slope stabilization work continuing at the site of a previous rockfall, about three kilometres west of town. Not fire-related, just another reminder that the highway network through this part of BC is under constant maintenance pressure.

00:09:35.686 --> 00:09:53.686
<v Riley>And on a lighter note to close out the roundup — the 100 Mile Free Press has Diana Forster previewing the Interlakes BCRA Rodeo, running on the upcoming August long weekend. Rodeo season keeps rolling on regardless of what's burning further south.

00:09:53.986 --> 00:10:13.700
<v Casey>Also worth a quick mention — Boing Boing flagged a small hiking tip making the rounds, that walking poles held behind you rather than planted in front burn about twenty percent more calories. Low stakes, but a nice one for anyone doing trail miles this summer.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:23.071
<v Riley>That's the roundup. Time to slow down for something a little more grounded before we get into the Deep Dive.

00:10:23.371 --> 00:10:56.799
<v Riley>If you've got kids looking for something to do this summer, Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is worth a look. It's volunteer-run through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and their summer programs rotate through a different theme every single week — Water one week, Bugs the next, Birds after that. There's also the Salmon in the Classroom program running for anyone curious about the Fraser's fish runs and what's happening to them. Sign-ups are at scoutisland.ca.

00:10:57.099 --> 00:11:09.956
<v Casey>Genuinely one of the better-run volunteer operations in this region — the kind of low-budget, high-value program that doesn't get nearly enough attention next to the bigger tourism marketing pushes.

00:11:10.756 --> 00:11:18.470
<v Riley>Speaking of what's actually happening to the wildlife on that river — that's exactly where we're headed next.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:33.341
<v Riley>Start with the obvious irony sitting underneath this whole region's name. This place is called the Cariboo. And in a lot of the mountain ranges it covers, actual caribou herds are close to gone.

00:11:33.641 --> 00:11:55.069
<v Casey>Not fire-related, mostly, at least not directly. Habitat fragmentation did most of the damage over decades — logging roads, snowmobile access opening up terrain, a predator-prey imbalance that followed from all of it. But this summer's fires are adding a new layer on top of populations that were already thin.

00:11:55.369 --> 00:12:12.511
<v Riley>Right, and that's the question worth sitting with today. Not the recreation-safety question we've covered before on this theme, and not the land-title governance question either. What's actually happening to the animals themselves, and what would genuinely protecting them require.

00:12:13.311 --> 00:12:20.596
<v Casey>Different question than we've asked so far on this theme. Good — that ground's been covered enough.

00:12:21.796 --> 00:12:51.795
<v Riley>Start with what technology can genuinely do here, because there is real capability. Motion-triggered wildlife cameras and GPS collar telemetry let biologists track exactly where herds move when fire changes the landscape underneath them — which drainages they abandon, which alpine refuges they retreat into. That kind of data used to take field crews years to assemble by hand. Now a lot of it comes in close to real time.

00:12:52.095 --> 00:13:00.237
<v Casey>Knowing where an animal went after its habitat burned doesn't rebuild the habitat. Monitoring is diagnosis. It isn't treatment.

00:13:00.537 --> 00:13:18.965
<v Riley>Diagnosis matters, though. If biologists know a herd shifted into one specific drainage after a fire, they can time logging deferrals or seasonal road closures to that exact area, instead of guessing across an entire range and spreading protection too thin to matter.

00:13:19.265 --> 00:13:41.550
<v Casey>Except that requires someone with actual authority to defer the logging, on a timeline that matches the animal's movement rather than the forestry company's cutting schedule. Data existing doesn't mean the decision follows it. We've made this point before on other stories — the gap between information and action doesn't close itself.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:14:14.064
<v Riley>Fair test, then — where has that gap actually closed? Farther north, in the Peace region, collaborative caribou recovery between provincial biologists and First Nations partners used maternal penning combined with real habitat protection to bring a nearly extirpated herd back from a few dozen animals to several times that number. It's not a Cariboo example, but it's proof the model can work when the data and the authority sit in the same hands.

00:14:14.364 --> 00:14:47.792
<v Casey>And it's also proof of how narrow that kind of win actually is. That recovery leaned heavily on predator management — wolf reduction — which is its own contested practice that plenty of conservationists have real problems with. And maternal penning is expensive, hands-on, intensive work that doesn't scale to every herd in every range. The herds around the Cariboo Mountains, Wells Gray, and Barkerville are arguably in worse shape than that Peace population was before intervention started.

00:14:48.992 --> 00:15:06.992
<v Riley>That's the harder admission to make, honestly. Some of these local herds may already be past the point where penning and predator management can pull them back. The Peace region recovery worked partly because intervention started before the population bottomed out completely.

00:15:07.292 --> 00:15:33.863
<v Casey>Which raises the question nobody in a provincial wildlife office wants to answer out loud — at what point do you stop calling something "recovery" and start calling it "management of a remnant population." That's not a technology question at all. That's an honesty question, and it's uncomfortable precisely because the honest answer probably closes off some funding and some public messaging.

00:15:34.663 --> 00:15:43.663
<v Riley>Moose are a cleaner case, though, for a completely different pressure — one that's climate-driven and genuinely under-monitored across this region.

00:15:43.963 --> 00:16:14.391
<v Casey>Winter ticks. Warmer, shorter winters mean tick populations survive that used to get killed off by a hard, sustained frost. A single moose can end up carrying thousands of ticks, losing blood and hair through the winter, sometimes dying from exposure and anemia by late winter or early spring — it's called "ghost moose syndrome" in some of the wildlife literature, because the hair loss leaves them looking pale and patchy.

00:16:14.691 --> 00:16:33.976
<v Riley>And there's no camera trap or GPS collar that fixes that particular problem. It's a climate problem showing up as a parasite problem, and the actual intervention — if there even is one — is a colder winter, which no provincial wildlife branch can engineer.

00:16:34.276 --> 00:16:45.418
<v Casey>Which is the honest limit of the tech-optimist case, right there. Monitoring tells you the herd is declining. It doesn't touch the cause underneath the decline.

00:16:45.718 --> 00:17:11.003
<v Riley>It's not nothing, though. Knowing tick load is climbing lets wildlife managers set hunting quotas that account for a population already under a stressor nobody controls, instead of managing blind and accidentally pushing a stressed population past its limit through overharvest. That's a real decision meaningfully improved by real data — just not a cure for the underlying problem.

00:17:11.803 --> 00:17:22.088
<v Casey>Agreed on that much. Diagnostic value, not curative value. Hold onto that distinction, because it changes how the fish story should be read too.

00:17:23.288 --> 00:17:51.573
<v Riley>The Fraser's salmon runs are under a version of that same squeeze this summer — drought lowering river flows, heat warming the water past what salmon can physically tolerate on their way upstream to spawn. Xatśūll First Nation has fisheries staff monitoring water temperature and flow on stretches of the Fraser near Soda Creek, and feeding that data into decisions about run timing and harvest levels.

00:17:51.873 --> 00:18:18.015
<v Casey>And the question is exactly the same shape as the caribou question. Who actually has the authority to act the moment a temperature reading crosses a lethal threshold for salmon? A monitoring buoy that flags a problem three days after a stretch of river has effectively cooked a run isn't protection — it's an obituary with better instrumentation attached to it.

00:18:18.315 --> 00:18:44.029
<v Riley>The instrumentation's genuinely improved on that front, though, and it matters. Real-time temperature loggers exist now that transmit hourly instead of requiring someone to physically retrieve a data card from a riverbank weeks later. That's the difference between finding out a run died in September, after the fact, and finding out mid-run, while there's still a window to adjust something.

00:18:44.329 --> 00:19:04.471
<v Casey>Mid-run adjustment requires someone with actual legal standing to close a fishery on short notice — and that's typically a federal DFO call, layered directly over a Nation's own fisheries authority. That overlapping jurisdiction moves slower, almost by design, than a heat spike on the river does.

00:19:05.271 --> 00:19:30.985
<v Riley>That's a real bottleneck, and it's worth naming specifically. It's also a narrower, more answerable question than "who owns the land," which is where a lot of these conversations usually end up stuck. This one is specifically about who holds emergency closure authority, and how fast that decision chain can actually move once the data says the water's become lethal.

00:19:31.285 --> 00:19:50.142
<v Casey>Narrower, sure. Still unresolved, though. Nobody's published a response-time standard for that decision chain — how many hours from a temperature alert to an actual closure order. Until that number exists, the instrumentation is measuring a problem faster than anyone can respond to it.

00:19:51.342 --> 00:20:21.770
<v Riley>There's a stranger footnote to all of this too, and it's worth putting on the table because it complicates the whole "better monitoring equals better understanding" argument. Orcas off the coast have apparently started ramming schools of fish hard enough to make them explode, using a second whale as a kind of battering setup against a sunfish held in the jaws of the first. Marine biologists only just documented the behavior.

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:23.355
<v Casey>That's genuinely wild.

00:20:23.655 --> 00:20:43.797
<v Riley>It is — and it's a decent humbling note for anyone who assumes monitoring technology means we fully understand these animals by now. We've had satellite tags on orca populations off this coast for years, and this behavior only just got caught on camera, apparently by accident.

00:20:44.097 --> 00:21:02.954
<v Casey>Which actually cuts against the tech-optimist case more than it supports it. More sensors and more tags doesn't guarantee more understanding — sometimes it's just luck, a research boat happening to be in the right place at the right moment with a camera running.

00:21:03.254 --> 00:21:23.396
<v Riley>Or it's evidence that more eyes on the water, over enough years, eventually catch things a single research vessel would've missed for decades. Both readings are consistent with the same fact — it's really a question of which one you find more persuasive as a baseline assumption.

00:21:24.196 --> 00:21:31.910
<v Casey>Fine. Let it sit as a genuinely open question rather than a point either of us wins today.

00:21:33.110 --> 00:22:06.110
<v Riley>So where does that leave the actual, concrete ask for a place like the Cariboo? Not another grant application, and not another short-term pilot program — a specific structural gap worth naming directly: real-time water temperature data on Fraser tributaries that's public and shared, rather than locked inside a single agency's internal system, so a local fisheries office, DFO, and any Nation monitoring that stretch of river can all see the same number at the same moment.

00:22:06.410 --> 00:22:29.124
<v Casey>That's a genuinely different ask than we've made on this theme before — it's not "who owns the authority," it's whether the information actually reaches every decision-maker who needs it simultaneously, instead of trickling through separate reporting chains at separate speeds and arriving too late for some of them to act on it.

00:22:29.424 --> 00:23:02.424
<v Riley>A community group could actually test a version of that fairly cheaply. A handful of low-cost temperature loggers placed on a tributary near Williams Lake or Quesnel, data posted publicly online, cross-checked against whatever Xatśūll's own monitoring already shows for that stretch of the Fraser. It wouldn't solve the jurisdiction problem. It would just prove that the same number can reach everyone who needs it, at the same time, without waiting on a formal data-sharing agreement first.

00:23:02.724 --> 00:23:26.295
<v Casey>Worth trying. And worth being honest that it doesn't touch the caribou side of this conversation at all. Some of those herds may not have a technology answer left on the table — just a harder, more uncomfortable conversation about what "recovery" even means at this point, and whether that word should still be used.

00:23:26.595 --> 00:23:57.452
<v Riley>Which might be the real takeaway to sit with today. The tools genuinely are getting better at telling us what's happening to these animals in something close to real time — the collar data, the temperature loggers, the camera traps. Whether that speed actually reaches the people with the authority to act on it — that's the gap this summer keeps exposing, herd by herd, run by run, all across this region.

00:23:58.252 --> 00:24:11.966
<v Casey>And if anyone listening is actually building something like that shared river-data setup, or has a correction on anything we got wrong today, the address is feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:24:12.266 --> 00:24:19.551
<v Riley>Enjoy your weekend, and try to keep the smoke out of your lungs while you're out there.

00:24:19.851 --> 00:24:21.565
<v Casey>We'll see you Monday.